+(duration)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a {class}`datetime.timedelta`. A value is either a whole
+number of seconds, such as `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d` (days), `h` (hours), `m`
+(minutes) and `s` (seconds), such as `2d`, `90m`, `1h30m` or `1d2h3m4s`. Each unit may appear at most once, units
+must be given in that order, and they are case-insensitive. Negative numbers, decimals and spaces are not accepted. A
+{class}`datetime.timedelta` passed as a value, for example as the `default`, is used unchanged.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    import datetime
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(),
+                  default=datetime.timedelta(minutes=5))
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds.")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--help'])
+```
+
+```{versionadded} 8.6
+```
+
